WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:03.440
Fairy tales.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.540
We grow up on them.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.700
Fantasy stories told to us as children with
a moral or a lesson made clear at the end.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.000
But those fairytales weren’t always as innocent
as we know them to be today.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.520
They were actually very dark, violent allegories
that scared children and adults alike.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.730
Take the cannibal witch from Hansel and Gretel.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.880
The trope of the evil old lady in a creepy
hut in the woods lying in wait for victims

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.540
is something we see often even outside of
the fairy tale genre

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:40.590
The gingerbread house, baking children in
ovens, the desire for human flesh, a secluded

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.630
location in the woods—while witches have
been associated with these characteristics

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:51.160
before, it wasn’t until brothers Jacob and
Wilhelm Grimm wrote it all down together that

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.619
this legendary monster was made.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:58.420
They collected their stories from oral folklore
accounts, so in order to truly understand

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:03.079
the origins of one of the most recognizable
tales today, we need to look further in history

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:07.180
- into the witch trials of Germany and the
famines that led people to make some very

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.620
desperate decisions.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.420
It’s a complex history.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.740
Speaking of which, stick around after the
episode for a special Monstrum announcement!

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.430
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.560
The original story of Hansel and Gretel was
first published in Germany in 1812, with the

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.480
first English translation in 1884.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:35.250
It tells the tale of two siblings, a boy and
a girl whose family has nothing left to eat.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.980
Their father, a poor woodcutter, is convinced
by the mother to abandon the children in the

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.030
deepest part of the forest to starve to death.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.750
Her philosophy is that it would be better
to have them killed by wild animals than to

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.560
watch them starve.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.729
The first attempt goes poorly.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:56.350
Hearing their parents’ plans for them, Hansel
sneaks out of the house to collect white stones.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:01.010
The next morning when the parents lead the
siblings deep into the woods, Hansel secretly

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:04.880
leaves a trail of these white pebbles back
to their house.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.119
Hansel and Gretel are told to build a fire
and that their parents will come back with

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:10.119
more wood.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:13.340
But when darkness falls, the parents had yet
to return.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.420
Luckily, the moon shines bright and the siblings
follow the pebble trail home, to the happiness

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.940
of their father and disappointment of their
mother.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.810
Again food runs out, and again the mother
insists the children should be abandoned.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.720
This time, Hansel uses a small amount of bread
to make another trail through the woods as

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:35.720
their parents walk them deeper into the dense
forest for the second time.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.810
The parents leave and night falls again, but
to their dismay, the children realize that

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.140
birds have eaten their breadcrumb trail.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.990
After three days of wandering in the woods,
they stumble upon a small house built entirely

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.810
of breads and cakes.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.850
The whole thing is covered with sweets, the
windows made of fine sugar.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.230
The starving Hansel and Gretel begin eating
chunks of the house when they are startled

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:05.220
by a little old woman who takes them inside
and serves them milk, fruit, and sweets.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.760
They fall asleep with full bellies.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.760
The old woman, it turns out, is a “wicked
witch”

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.810
who built her house to lure children
in so she could kill and eat them.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:20.280
When the children wake she throws Hansel into
a small chicken coop and she forces Gretel

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.040
to do chores, including feeding her brother
to fatten him up for slaughter.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.690
Hansel manages to prolong his demise by poking
out a chicken bone for the witch to feel when

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.570
she checks his fatness.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:36.849
After four weeks, the witch tells Gretel to
check the bread baking in the oven, secretly

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.030
intending to push the child in and bake her.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.940
But Gretel manages to outsmart the witch by
tricking her into looking into the oven herself.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.950
As soon as the old woman steps inside, Gretel
quickly locks her in.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.050
Gretel frees Hansel as the witch screams.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:54.910
Before they flee, they steal her precious
stones and pearls.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:58.760
The children manage to find their way home
and make their father a rich man.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.050
Their mother has died.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.770
Abandoning children and baking an old lady
in an oven.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.319
What a pleasant story.

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.500
What could possibly have prompted the creation
of this twisted tale?

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.500
Well, we asked our friend Lindsay found some
interesting theories...

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.660
Well, for one thing the 1810 manuscript written
by Jacob and Wilhelm, constructed a version

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:19.939
of the Hansel and Gretel story from one told
by Dortchen Wild, who would eventually become

00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:21.430
Wilhelm’s wife.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.710
Although Dortchen grew up in Germany, she
had French ancestry.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:30.259
Which might explain why the story bears quite
a few similarities to the 17th century French

00:04:30.259 --> 00:04:33.870
fairytale “Le Petit Poucet” by Charles
Perrault.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.719
The story includes a poor woodcutter who cannot
feed his children, so he and his wife abandon

00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:40.629
them, despite her protests.

00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:44.729
The smallest of the children saves himself
and his siblings from the woods once with

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:47.330
pebbles and tries to again with breadcrumbs.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.870
An evil cannibal ogre tries to eat them before
accidentally killing his own daughters instead.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:57.470
What I’m really interested in is where the
stories diverge.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.969
The change of a male ogre into a female witch
for one.

00:05:00.969 --> 00:05:04.509
And why do the Grimm Brothers add in the house
made from bread?

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:07.750
Why would they have presented the mother as
the murderous parent?

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.350
I have some theories, but first we need to
address the element of the story that starts

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.470
the whole thing off—the willing abandonment
of one’s own children.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:20.539
In the 18th century and early 19th century,
Germany suffered from multiple famines.

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:28.710
It was estimated there was a 40% population
loss to starvation and disease between 1708-1711.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.879
Another famine hit in 1771-72.

00:05:31.879 --> 00:05:37.979
The Grimm brothers were born in 1785 and 1786
so they might have felt or seen the lingering

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:42.789
effects of these losses, or at the very least,
encountered older people in their folklore

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:45.389
interviews who lived through these events.

00:05:45.389 --> 00:05:49.389
One of the realities of famine at this time
was that children might be abandoned or even

00:05:49.389 --> 00:05:53.199
killed because there was not enough food to
go around.

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:57.389
Some tried to justify these deaths, believing
they helped ensure the survival of the family

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:58.659
as a whole.

00:05:58.659 --> 00:06:03.199
It can be argued the Hansel and Gretel story
served as a way to rationalize such dramatic,

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:05.039
horrific measures.

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:10.099
During the second half of the 18th century,
it was illegal for mothers not to report their

00:06:10.099 --> 00:06:11.339
pregnancies.

00:06:11.339 --> 00:06:16.199
All other individuals were obligated to report
any suspected pregnancies.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:20.990
Some women believed hiding pregnancy was necessary
for their economic, social, and even personal

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.080
survival.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.789
Court records from infanticide trials during
this time support that, naming “poverty,

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:31.750
fear of punishment, and fear of social shame”
as the primary given motives.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.599
Interestingly, even though both fathers and
mothers could be accused of murdering their

00:06:36.599 --> 00:06:41.149
children, it was only mothers who were charged
with infanticide.

00:06:41.149 --> 00:06:46.210
All of this historical context might explain
why it is the mother in the 1812 Grimm story

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.150
who insists on abandoning the children.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.020
So what about the wicked witch?

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:55.569
Germany had its own unique interpretation
of an evil cannibal monster.

00:06:55.569 --> 00:07:00.240
The “Kinderlfresser” or “child-devourer”
served as both a fairy tale and warning to

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.259
children.

00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:06.460
These ghoulish creatures were usually described
as large, hairy men with a sack full of children

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:11.479
it would greedily stuff into its mouth—a
surefire way to keep kids obedient.

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:15.461
The female version of this monster, [Butzen-Bercht,]
resembled the stereotypical witch: an old

00:07:15.461 --> 00:07:18.430
hunchback with a hooked nose and messy hair.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:23.119
So perhaps because of this, when people in
Germany were prosecuted for witchcraft they

00:07:23.119 --> 00:07:27.389
were often also accused of kidnapping and
eating children.

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:31.300
The whole “baking the kids in the oven”
aspect of Hansel and Gretel may even have

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.229
its own explanation.

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:37.559
In my research, I stumbled across a series
of German witch trials in the 16th century

00:07:37.559 --> 00:07:41.279
[Nördlingen trials] where 35 supposed witches
were sentenced.

00:07:41.279 --> 00:07:45.629
Under torture, some of them confessed to child
cannibalism and “cooking infant flesh in

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:47.129
copper pots.”

00:07:47.129 --> 00:07:52.759
They also even claimed “to process the dead
children’s bodies and bake themn in ovens.”

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:53.949
Sound familiar?

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:58.409
So we’ve talked about the witch, but what
about the house made from bread and sweets?

00:07:58.409 --> 00:08:02.389
The words gingerbread house are an English
translation of the German word [Pfefferkuchenhaus].

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:08.069
From what I could find, the Brothers Grimm
were the first people to write this into a

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:09.270
story with a witch.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.509
They made the real-world gingerbread house
popular.

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:15.849
Or rather, an opera about Hansel and Gretel
did.

00:08:15.849 --> 00:08:20.560
Overtime the house evolved, becoming a famous
icon largely because of Engelbert Humperdinck’s

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:26.099
operatic interpretation of the fairy tale,
first performed in December 1893.

00:08:26.099 --> 00:08:30.430
The house is described made of cake and referred
to as the gingerbread house.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:35.020
The set design for the opera reinforced the
image; it made something that had only existed

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.390
in the imagination more concrete.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:43.120
In addition to being made from cake, the house
was surrounded by a wall of gingerbread children,

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:44.820
the victims of the wicked witch.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:50.340
In other words, he toned down the overt cannibalism
and upped the aesthetic factor.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:54.820
The Brothers Grimm took oral tradition and
hundreds of years of German folklore and made

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.120
it into one of the most popular stories of
all time, that continues to frighten children

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.510
to this day.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:04.580
So when you’re constructing a house from
cookies and frosting and watching questionable

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:11.000
decisions by great actors, remember that some
things have a deeper and darker meaning.

